{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>וְאַתָּה תְּצַוֶּה אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וגו׳.</b> One must examine: what is the meaning of ״וְאַתָּה תְּצַוֶּה״ — two second-person forms? He should have said ״וְאַתָּה צַו״. Moreover, the word ״וְאַתָּה״ is entirely superfluous, for he should have said ״צַו אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, for in this language it is stated in parashat Emor (Leviticus 24:2) concerning these lamps. And what the Ramban wrote, to say that there was a insistence that this not be done through an agent, etc. — nevertheless this itself is a difficulty: why was he insistent regarding this commandment more than regarding all the other commandments? And so, nearby it says ״וְאַתָּה תְּדַבֵּר״ — there too the matter requires resolution. The commentators further asked: what is the relevance of this commandment here, for its principal place is after the making of the Tabernacle and the setting up of the menorah? And Maharhi Abravanel explained that the principal commandment is in parashat Emor, and here ״תְּצַוֶּה״ is said, meaning: you are destined to command the children of Israel, etc. And according to his words this entire verse is superfluous, for what did he tell him by this — that in the end he would command? And what would the master do with the verse ״וְאַתָּה תְּדַבֵּר״ said nearby? A further difficulty: ״לְהַעֲלֹת נֵר תָּמִיד״ — he should say ״נֵרוֹת״, since there were seven. A further difficulty: did they not burn only from evening to morning, so why did he say ״תָּמִיד״? A further difficulty: when he says ״בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד מִחוּץ לַפָּרֹכֶת אֲשֶׁר עַל הָעֵדֻת״, all this lengthy description of place appears unnecessary, for he should have said ״עַל הַמְּנוֹרָה יַעֲרֹךְ אוֹתוֹ״, and we already know the place of the menorah where it is. And here only one lamp is mentioned, whereas in parashat Emor he says ״יַעֲרֹךְ אֶת הַנֵּרוֹת״. Furthermore, here it says ״בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ and afterward ״מִחוּץ לַפָּרֹכֶת״, whereas in parashat Emor the order is reversed, saying first ״מִחוּץ לְפָרֹכֶת״ and afterward ״בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״. And here it says ״מִחוּץ לַפָּרֹכֶת אֲשֶׁר עַל הָעֵדֻת״, whereas there it says ״מִחוּץ לְפָרֹכֶת הָעֵדֻת״." "And it appears possible to resolve all of this from what the Ramban wrote, that what is said ״לְהֲעֲלֹת נֵר תָּמִיד״ refers to the western lamp, which burned perpetually; but the other lamps were not perpetual, only from evening until morning. On this assumption all the doubts mentioned will be resolved. For it says that HaShem commanded Moses concerning the Tabernacle and all its vessels, and the Tabernacle was divided into three parts, and concerning all of them He said ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם״ (Exodus 25:8). And the curtain separated between the Holy and the Holy of Holies, and Moses wanted that in every part there would be something testifying that the Shekhinah dwells among Israel. Now, in the Holy of Holies there were the tablets of testimony, which would give ״עֵדוּת ה׳ נֶאֱמָנָה״ (Psalms 19:8) that the Shekhinah dwells there, for the tablets had in them a miraculous quality — they were called מִשְּׁנֵי עֶבְרֵיהֶם (readable from both sides), and the מִ״ם and סָמֶ״ךְ in the tablets stood by miracle (Shabbat 104a; Megillah 3a). And it goes without saying that all who came to that place would see eye to eye that the name of HaShem is called upon it. However, since the curtain intervenes and not every person is permitted to enter there, lest the skeptic say — ״הָרוֹצֶה לְשַׁקֵּר יַרְחִיק עֵדָיו״ (one who wishes to lie distances his witnesses — Rosh, Shevu'ot HaDayanim 13) — therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to make also in the Sanctuary this perpetual miracle through the western lamp, so that all would gaze upon it and see from the western lamp the name of HaShem, that He dwells in their midst. And had the curtain not covered the ark of the testimony, there would have been no need for this miracle." "And according to this proposal, we may say that the entire intention of this passage is not to command concerning the lamps of the menorah but only in parashat Emor, where the principal place of this commandment is; rather, here the principal command concerns this western lamp, to reveal to Moses that even though the curtain covers the ark of the testimony, nevertheless there will also be in the place of the Holy a perpetual miracle, proclaiming that HaShem dwells there. And you already know that Moses our master, peace be upon him, it is said of him (Deuteronomy 33:21) ״וַיַּרְא רֵאשִׁית לוֹ״, for he was the first and beginning of all things that stand by miracle, and he was like this lamp from which many lamps are kindled and the lamp lacks nothing. So did he bestow from himself an abundance of the spirit of holy prophecy upon all the prophets who came after him, for ״פְּנֵי מֹשֶׁה כַחַמָּה״ (Bava Batra 75a), and from him the light was bestowed upon them all. So too this western lamp, whose light was perpetual by way of miracle — it drew from the light of Moses, for he was the intermediary between the light of HaShem and every kind of light that stood by miracle. And the sign for this was that at his birth the entire house was filled with light, to indicate that he would be the first receiver of the supernal light, and through his mediation the light was bestowed upon every place where there is a thing that stands by miracle. Therefore it says ״וְאַתָּה תְּצַוֶּה״ — two second-person forms — to say: ״וְאַתָּה״ — that is, from the essence of your very being and the additional light that is in you — ״תְּצַוֶּה אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ שֶׁמֶן״ etc., that the oil is taken to you, meaning, to join it to you in order that you bestow upon it from your splendor, in order ״לְהַעֲלֹת נֵר תָּמִיד״ — this is the western lamp, which was perpetual by way of miracle." "And afterward he gave a reason in praise — why there was need for this miracle: because it is בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד מִחוּץ לַפָּרֹכֶת אֲשֶׁר עַל הָעֵדוּת, for the curtain separates between the place where there is testimony that the Shekhinah dwells among Israel and between the ordinary Tent of Meeting. Therefore ״יַעֲרֹךְ אֹתוֹ אַהֲרֹן מֵעֶרֶב עַד בֹּקֶר״ (Exodus 27:21) — and was he standing and arranging it the whole night? Rather, he puts into it oil in the measure of its companion lamps, so that it contains only enough to burn from evening until morning, and even so ״יַעֲלֶה נֵר תָּמִיד״ — it would remain lit perpetually. And he still had not specified in which lamp this miracle would be perpetual, until he said ״לִפְנֵי ה׳״ (Exodus 27:21) — that is, the western lamp, which is adjacent to the Shekhinah that is in the west, for from the side of testimony this lamp resembles the ark of the testimony, and ״עַל פִּי שְׁנַיִם עֵדִים יָקוּם דָּבָר״ (Deuteronomy 19:15). And both of them come through Moses, for the Torah too is called by his name. And he did not mention all the lamps here, for their place is not here but only in parashat Emor, as it is written there: ״עַל הַמְּנֹרָה הַטְּהֹרָה יַעֲרֹךְ אֶת הַנֵּרוֹת״ (Leviticus 24:4). And therefore ״וְאַתָּה תְּצַוֶּה״ is not said there, because the principal command there was concerning all the lamps, and incidentally it made mention there also of this western lamp, in saying ״לְהַעֲלֹת נֵר תָּמִיד מִחוּץ לְפָרֹכֶת הָעֵדֻת בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד יַעֲרֹךְ אֹתוֹ״ (Leviticus 24:3–4)." "And it appears to me that the order of the verse in parashat Emor is as follows: one must separate between the word ״לְפָרֹכֶת״ and the word ״הָעֵדֻת״, and this is its meaning: ״לְהַעֲלֹת נֵר תָּמִיד מִחוּץ לַפָּרֹכֶת״ — HaShem wanted there to be one perpetual lamp by way of miracle, outside the curtain; and afterward he explained what this miracle serves — concerning this he said ״הָעֵדֻת בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״, for this miracle would give עֵדוּת ה׳ נֶאֱמָנָה also in the Tent of Meeting, where all gaze there and see from this western lamp the name of HaShem, that He is in their midst — for ״יַעֲרֹךְ אֹתוֹ אַהֲרֹן מֵעֶרֶב עַד בֹּקֶר״ and it will be ״לִפְנֵי ה׳ תָּמִיד״. And since it says there ״צַו אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְיִקְחוּ אֵלֶיךָ״, hear from this that there is here a thing attributed to all the children of Israel and there is in it a thing attributed to Moses alone, for behold it says ״אֵלֶיךָ״. Therefore he needed to explain both: for the thing attributed ״אֵלֶיךָ״ and not to them is the thing that stands by miracle, concerning which he said ״לְהַעֲלֹת נֵר תָּמִיד… חֻקַּת עוֹלָם לְדֹרֹתֵיכֶם״. And afterward he explained the thing attributed to all Israel, which is what he said: ״עַל הַמְּנֹרָה הַטְּהֹרָה יַעֲרֹךְ אֶת הַנֵּרוֹת לִפְנֵי ה׳ תָּמִיד״, for all the lamps faced toward this western lamp; therefore concerning all of them it says ״לִפְנֵי ה׳ תָּמִיד״. And this interpretation is precious." "<b>Another thing:</b> therefore it specifies this location — ״outside the veil that is before the testimony.״ This is so that one should not err and say that He, blessed be He, needs its light, since the veil interposes between the menorah and the place of the indwelling of His Shekhinah. And furthermore, there — there is the testimony, and all of it is light; rather, surely this taking of oil is ״to you״ — for your need. And in the Yalkut (Shim'oni §780) it concludes that therefore the windows of the Temple were broad on the outside and narrow on the inside, to demonstrate that the sun did not illuminate the Temple, but rather the Temple illuminated the whole world, as it is said (Ezekiel 43:2) ״and the earth was illuminated by His glory.״ And so we explained above in the portion of Vayetze (Genesis 28:11) on the verse ״and he encountered the place and lodged there because the sun had set.״ And perhaps the reason it specifies ״to you״ is as an amplification: for even the face of Moses, which is like the face of the sun, nevertheless you need this spiritual light — for the sun does not illuminate the Temple, but the Temple illuminates the sun, for from it the effluence goes forth to all the host on high." "And there is in this passage a moral lesson for those who stray, who give the choicest portion of their wealth for physical needs — to eat and drink and the rest of human pleasures — while all the work that is despised and contemptible, that they give for the purpose of ״the lamp of YHWH is the soul of man״ (Proverbs 20:27), in the manner that Cain did. But one should walk in the way of Abel, who brought an offering to YHWH ״from the firstborn of his flock and from their fat״ (Genesis 4:4). So too one should give the first to the luminary, for the purpose of ״the lamp of YHWH is the soul of man,״ whose light is eternal, as it is written ״to cause the lamp to burn continually״ (Exodus 27:20), and the second one should give for the meal-offerings, which were food for the priests. And a hint suffices for the discerning."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {3 "In the phrase ״יַעֲלֶה נֵר תָּמִיד״ the text shifts from the word ״לְהַעֲלֹת״ in the biblical verse to ״יַעֲלֶה״ ('it shall burn/remain lit'), apparently switching to a descriptive statement about the lamp's miraculous persistence; rendered as 'it would remain lit perpetually.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:28.044276704Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}